{"timestamp":"2026-06-06T01:28:01.333Z","type":"tool","step":"IDLE","details":{"toolName":"task","args":{"description":"Admin guard tightening + headers","prompt":"You are a subagent working on a small security hardening task in the AerialDB repo at `/Users/bbaaxx/Code/projects/aerialdb`.\n\n## Objective\n\nPort the unique value from closed PRs #135 and #141 (which were closed as duplicates of merged #138) to a new branch off dev. The changes are:\n\n1. **Admin guard path tightening** in `src/hooks.server.ts`. Change:\n   ```ts\n   if (event.url.pathname.startsWith('/admin')) {\n   ```\n   to:\n   ```ts\n   const path = event.url.pathname;\n   if (path === '/admin' || path.startsWith('/admin/')) {\n   ```\n   This prevents accidental matches on routes like `/administration` that share the `/admin` prefix.\n\n2. **Add `X-Permitted-Cross-Domain-Policies: none`** to the security headers in the same file. Add a line like:\n   ```ts\n   response.headers.set('X-Permitted-Cross-Domain-Policies', 'none');\n   ```\n   next to the other response.headers.set calls in `handleSecurityHeaders`.\n\n3. **Expand `Permissions-Policy`** in the same file. The current string is `'geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=()'`. Change it to add `usb=(), interest-cohort=(), screen-wake-lock=()`. Final string: `'geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=(), interest-cohort=(), screen-wake-lock=()'`.\n\n4. **Update `src/hooks.server.security.spec.ts`** — find the `expectedHeaders` test object and:\n   - Add `'x-permitted-cross-domain-policies': 'none'` (or whatever key format the test uses — match the existing pattern).\n   - Update the `permissions-policy` value to match the expanded string.\n\n## Setup\n\n- Work in a worktree: `git worktree add /tmp/opencode-worktrees/admin-guard -b admin-guard-tightening origin/dev`\n- Inside the worktree: `cd /tmp/opencode-worktrees/admin-guard`\n- Read the current state of `src/hooks.server.ts` and `src/hooks.server.security.spec.ts` BEFORE editing (the post-merge state already has #138's HSTS preload, Clear-Site-Data cache, and CSP — work on top of that).\n\n## Verification\n\nRun from the worktree:\n- `npm run lint` — must remain clean for code files. (Pre-existing mdocs/.Jules warnings are OK.)\n- `npm run test:unit -- --run` — all 115 tests should pass.\n- `npm run check` — should NOT have new errors. (It has 7 pre-existing spec errors that aren't from this change — those are out of scope for this task.)\n\nIf any of these fail unexpectedly, fix the issue or report it in your final message.\n\n## Commit\n\nAfter verification passes, commit your changes with a clear message:\n```\nchore: tighten admin guard path matching and harden security headers\n\nPorts unique value from closed PRs #135 and #141:\n- Tighten admin guard from `pathname.startsWith('/admin')` to\n  `pathname === '/admin' || pathname.startsWith('/admin/')` to\n  prevent accidental matches on `/administration` and similar routes.\n- Add `X-Permitted-Cross-Domain-Policies: none` to security headers.\n- Expand `Permissions-Policy` to disable `usb`, `interest-cohort`,\n  and `screen-wake-lock` (defense-in-depth).\n- Update expectedHeaders in security spec to match.\n```\n\n**Do NOT push to origin.** I will review and push/merge.\n\n## Return format\n\nYour final message must contain:\n1. **Branch name:** `admin-guard-tightening`\n2. **Worktree path:** `/tmp/opencode-worktrees/admin-guard`\n3. **Commit SHA:** (output of `git rev-parse HEAD` in the worktree)\n4. **Files changed:** list with line counts (output of `git diff --stat origin/dev`)\n5. **Verification results:** literal output (last 5-10 lines) of `npm run lint`, `npm run test:unit -- --run`, and `npm run check`\n6. **Any anomalies or notes** — e.g., if the spec file used a different key format than expected.","subagent_type":"CoderAgent"}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-06-06T01:29:31.548Z","type":"tool","step":"IDLE","details":{"toolName":"task","args":{"description":"Agent-spawn pattern mitigation plan","prompt":"You are a subagent researching and proposing architectural changes to the AerialDB agent/PR workflow. The repo is at `/Users/bbaaxx/Code/projects/aerialdb`. This is **research and proposal only** — do NOT make any code changes.\n\n## Background\n\nAerialDB uses three named coding agents (Bolt for performance, Sentinel for security, Palette for UI) driven by Google Jules tasks. When parallel Jules tasks tackle the same high-level goal, they produce near-identical PRs that compete for the same changes. A \"Guardian\" agent verifies PRs on CI/lint/test, but does not check for semantic duplication. This pattern recurs every 1–2 weeks.\n\nThe wiki entry at `mdocs/wiki/process/agent-pr-spawn-pattern.md` documents the pattern and proposes 4 mitigations:\n\n1. **Single-task-per-topic convention for Jules** — prevent parallel/redundant Jules tasks from being dispatched.\n2. **Semantic-duplication check in Guardian** — when a new PR is opened, compare its file set and commit subject against all open PRs and flag suspected duplicates.\n3. **Branch naming convention with retry suffix** — e.g., `-v2`, `-v3` for retry attempts, making duplicate detection trivial.\n4. **Defensive defaults in CSP template** — `style-src` should include `'unsafe-inline'` by default (required by Svelte transitions), with a comment explaining the constraint, so future CSP-tightening PRs are required to remove all `transition:` directives or fail review.\n\nThe June 2026 incident (recently completed) is documented in the wiki and in the closed `pr-triage-2026-06-03` initiative at `mdocs/initiatives/pr-triage-2026-06-03--2026-06-04.md`.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. **Read** the wiki entry `mdocs/wiki/process/agent-pr-spawn-pattern.md` and the closed initiative `mdocs/initiatives/pr-triage-2026-06-03--2026-06-04.md` for full context.\n\n2. **Investigate the Guardian agent.** Find where its source lives (likely under `.opencode/agent/`, `.claude/`, or referenced from `.opencode/opencode.jsonc`). Determine:\n   - File path(s) of the Guardian agent definition.\n   - Its current verification logic (what it checks, what hooks it uses).\n   - Whether it has a hook point for \"before merge\" checks that could be extended.\n   - How it accesses GitHub PR data (the `gh` CLI, the GitHub API, a webhook, etc.).\n   - If there is no existing Guardian, look for any \"PR verification\" or \"automated PR review\" agent. If nothing exists, note that.\n\n3. **Investigate the Jules task dispatch flow.** Look at:\n   - `.Jules/bolt.md`, `.Jules/sentinel.md`, `.Jules/palette.md` (agent instructions for Jules).\n   - Any scripts or hooks that dispatch Jules tasks.\n   - Whether the task IDs in PR bodies (e.g., `*PR created automatically by Jules for task [...]`) trace back to a queue or scheduler that could enforce single-task-per-topic.\n\n4. **Investigate the CSP setup.** Look at:\n   - `svelte.config.js` for the current CSP configuration.\n   - Search for all `transition:` usages in `.svelte` files (the repo currently has 5).\n   - Whether there's a CI check that fails PRs that tighten `style-src` while using `transition:` directives.\n\n5. **For each of the 4 mitigations**, produce a 1-page section in your final report:\n   - **Mitigation X: <name>**\n   - **Where the change would go:** specific file paths and line numbers (or new files to create).\n   - **What the change would look like:** code sketch (TypeScript / shell / config), 5-20 lines, no need to be production-ready — just enough to show the shape.\n   - **Tradeoffs and risks:** e.g., false positives in duplication detection, branch-naming breaking existing automation, CSP template changes affecting current behavior.\n   - **Estimated effort:** small (< 1 day) / medium (1-3 days) / large (> 3 days).\n\n6. **Rank the 4 mitigations by ROI** and recommend which one(s) to implement first. Consider:\n   - Impact: how much duplication would the mitigation prevent?\n   - Cost: how much code/config to maintain?\n   - Risk: what could go wrong?\n   - Discoverability: can the user see the mitigation working (e.g., Guardian flagging a duplicate)?\n\n7. **Propose a 2-week implementation roadmap** for the recommended mitigations, with concrete deliverables.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Do NOT modify any source files. Read-only research.\n- You may create scratch notes in `/tmp/opencode-worktrees/agent-spawn-research/` if you want a scratch directory for working notes (it's not required).\n- Do NOT push anything.\n\n## Return format\n\nYour final message must be a complete markdown report with these sections:\n\n```\n# Agent-Spawn Pattern Mitigation Plan\n\n## Executive Summary\n(2-3 sentences: what's recommended, why, expected impact)\n\n## Guardian Investigation Findings\n(what you found about the Guardian agent's source, hooks, and current checks)\n\n## Mitigation 1: Single-task-per-topic convention for Jules\n- Where: ...\n- Sketch: ...\n- Tradeoffs: ...\n- Effort: ...\n\n## Mitigation 2: Semantic-duplication check in Guardian\n(same structure)\n\n## Mitigation 3: Branch naming with retry suffix\n(same structure)\n\n## Mitigation 4: Defensive CSP template defaults\n(same structure)\n\n## ROI Ranking\n(ordered list with brief justification per mitigation)\n\n## Recommended 2-Week Roadmap\n(week-by-week plan with concrete deliverables and verification criteria)\n\n## Open Questions\n(any ambiguities the user should resolve before implementation)\n```\n\nBe specific. Cite file paths and line numbers. If a mitigation requires a tool/library that doesn't exist yet, name it. If you find a constraint that makes a mitigation infeasible, say so clearly.","subagent_type":"general"}}}
